      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA multiplication table is a map of answers. Each line shows what happens when we add the same number again and again. The number we add is one **factor**, the number of times we add it is the other **factor**, and the answer is the **product**.\n\nIn the five texts listed above, we built the ten tables from groups we can see and touch: empty plates, fingers, triangles, squares, flower petals, days of the week, octagons and enneagons. In this summary we walk through those tables **one section per table, from 0 to 9**. Each section opens with an invitation to count and builds on the tables before it. The idea is always the same: **multiplying is a fast way of adding the same number many times**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Same product, different order</b><br /><br />Pause and think: why do 4 × 5 and 5 × 4 give the same product? Try to explain it using groups of objects.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: 4 × 5 means four groups of five, and 5 × 4 means five groups of four. Both arrangements contain twenty objects, so both products are 20. This is the **commutative property** of multiplication: changing the order of the factors does not change the product. We will see this property again in every pair of tables we review (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_zero",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Zero\n\nWe begin with the shortest table of all: the table of zero. Multiplying by zero always gives zero, because zero groups of anything contain nothing, as the empty plate in Figure 1 shows:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 240\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:240px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An empty plate\"><title>An empty plate</title><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"222\" rx=\"88\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#000000\" opacity=\"0.12\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"100\" fill=\"#f7f7f7\" stroke=\"#c9c9c9\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"76\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#dcdcdc\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"116\" r=\"60\" fill=\"#fdfdfd\" stroke=\"#e6e6e6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><ellipse cx=\"86\" cy=\"82\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#ffffff\" opacity=\"0.8\" transform=\"rotate(-25 86 82)\"/></svg><b>Figure 1</b>: An empty plate.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of zero:** 0 × N = 0. For example, 0 × 9 = 0 and 12 × 0 = 0.\n\nThis rule works no matter how large N is. It also works when the order changes: 9 × 0 = 0 × 9 = 0.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_one",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of One\n\nNow, let us count 1 by 1. Multiplying by one keeps the number unchanged: one group of N is still N.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th></tr> <tr id=\"tr0\"><td><img id=\"sumHandOneL\" alt=\"Left hand with one finger raised, showing how to count by ones; click to zoom the image\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHandOneL', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"sumHandOneR\" alt=\"Right hand with one finger raised, showing how to count by ones; click to zoom the image\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHandOneR', '0%')\" /></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Counting on our fingers to understand multiplication by 1.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of one:** 1 × N = N. For example, 1 × 7 = 7 and 20 × 1 = 20.\n\nFigure 2 shows one finger raised on each hand: this is how we count by ones to understand multiplication by 1.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "These two rules — zero and one — are the foundation of all the other tables. They also work when the order changes: 7 × 1 = 1 × 7 = 7. With that foundation ready, we can start counting by larger numbers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_two",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Two\n\nNow, let us count 2 by 2. Multiplying by two means adding the number twice, so the product is the **double** of the number. Figure 3 shows two hands, each with five fingers: two groups of five give 10.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"sumHand2L\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHand2L', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"sumHand2R\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHand2R', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: Two hands, each with five fingers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Two hands, each with five fingers.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of two:** 2 × N = N + N. Counting by twos gives 2, 4, 6, 8, 10...\n\nFor example, 2 × 6 = 6 + 6 = 12.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A useful shortcut: because 2 × N is the double of N, every product in the table of two is an **even number**. Next, we add one more group.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_three",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Three\n\nNow, let us count 3 by 3. Multiplying by three means adding the number three times. Figure 4 shows three triangles of the same size: each triangle has three sides, so three groups of three give 9.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 440 180\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three equilateral triangles of the same size\"><g fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"20,160 120,160 70,73.4\"/><polygon points=\"170,160 270,160 220,73.4\"/><polygon points=\"320,160 420,160 370,73.4\"/></g><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><line x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"156.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"164.0\"/><line x1=\"91.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"98.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/><line x1=\"48.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"41.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/><line x1=\"220.0\" y1=\"156.0\" x2=\"220.0\" y2=\"164.0\"/><line x1=\"241.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"248.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/><line x1=\"198.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"191.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/><line x1=\"370.0\" y1=\"156.0\" x2=\"370.0\" y2=\"164.0\"/><line x1=\"391.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"398.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/><line x1=\"348.5\" y1=\"118.7\" x2=\"341.5\" y2=\"114.7\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 4</b>: Three triangles of the same size, each with three equal sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Three triangles of the same size, each with three equal sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of three:** 3 × N = N + N + N. Counting by threes gives 3, 6, 9, 12, 15...\n\nFor example, 3 × 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 = 12. Notice that 2 × 3 = 3 × 2 = 6: the order of the factors does not change the product.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The doubles and triples are ready. Let us move on to squares and hands.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_four",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Four\n\nNow, let us count 4 by 4. A square has four sides, and a rectangle has four sides too, as Figure 5 shows. These groups help us build the table of four:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 430 150\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A square and a rectangle\"><g stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"30\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"20\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 5</b>: A square (left) and a rectangle (right). Both have four sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 550 140\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five squares of the same size\"><g fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"450\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 6</b>: Five squares of the same size, each with four equal sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of four:** 4 × N = N + N + N + N. Counting by fours gives 4, 8, 12, 16, 20...\n\nFigure 6 shows five squares of the same size: five groups of four sides give 20. For example, 4 × 5 = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A shortcut: because 4 = 2 × 2, the table of four is the table of two doubled. Now for a table many people find handy — the table of five.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_five",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Five\n\nNow, let us count 5 by 5. A hand has five fingers, and Figure 7 shows two hands: two groups of five give 10.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"sumHand5L\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHand5L', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"sumHand5R\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('sumHand5R', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 7</b>: Two hands, each with five fingers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of five:** 5 × N = N + N + N + N + N. Counting by fives gives 5, 10, 15, 20, 25...\n\nFor example, 5 × 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20. Once again, 4 × 5 = 5 × 4.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A shortcut: every product in the table of five ends in 0 or 5. From hands and squares we now turn to flowers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_six",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Six\n\nNow, let us count 6 by 6. Figure 8 shows a flower with six petals around its center: each petal is one unit of the group of six.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 230\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A flower with six petals around its yellow center\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 8</b>: A flower with six petals around its center. Each petal is one unit of our group of six.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 240\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals\"><g><g transform=\"translate(-120 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#f8bbd0\" stroke=\"#ad1457\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(10 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#bbdefb\" stroke=\"#1565c0\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(140 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#ffe0b2\" stroke=\"#ef6c00\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(270 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#e1bee7\" stroke=\"#6a1b9a\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(400 0)\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M180 110 L180 216\"/><path d=\"M180 170 Q148 152 136 168 Q156 188 180 182\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/><path d=\"M180 190 Q212 172 224 188 Q204 206 180 202\" fill=\"#a5d6a7\"/></g><g fill=\"#c8e6c9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\"><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(60 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(120 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(180 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(240 180 110)\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"32\" transform=\"rotate(300 180 110)\"/></g><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#ffe082\" stroke=\"#f57f17\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 9</b>: Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Five flowers of different colors, each with six petals.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of six:** 6 × N = N + N + N + N + N + N. Counting by sixes gives 6, 12, 18, 24, 30...\n\nFigure 9 shows five flowers, each with six petals: five groups of six give 30. A shortcut: because 6 = 3 × 2, the table of six is the table of three doubled.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Next comes the table that matches the days of a week.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_seven",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Seven\n\nNow, let us count 7 by 7. Figure 10 shows the seven days of a week: each day is one unit of the group of seven.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"htCalendarFigureWrap\"><div id=\"htSumWeekCalendarFigure\" class=\"htCalendarFigure\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Seven-day calendar\"></div></div><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 10</b>: The seven days of a week, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page. Each day is one unit of our group of seven.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "A seven-day week, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"htCalendarFigureWrap\"><div id=\"htSumFourWeekCalendarFigure\" class=\"htCalendarFigure\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Four-week calendar\"></div></div><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 11</b>: A calendar with four weeks, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "A four-week calendar, using the day names of the calendar selected at the top of the page.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of seven:** 7 × N = N + N + N + N + N + N + N. Counting by sevens gives 7, 14, 21, 28, 35...\n\nFor example, 7 × 6 = 42. Notice that 6 × 7 = 42 as well: the order of the factors does not change the product. Figure 11 shows four weeks: 4 × 7 = 28 days.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A shortcut: each product in the table of seven is the matching product in the table of six plus N, so 7 × 5 = 30 + 5 = 35. Two tables remain — the polygons will help us with them.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_eight",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Eight\n\nNow, let us count 8 by 8. An octagon has eight sides. Figure 12 shows polygons with three to nine sides so you can compare them; Figure 13 shows one octagon with its 8 sides.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 330 440\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Polygons with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides\"><g stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"60.0,36.0 89.4,87.0 30.6,87.0\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,36.0 199.0,70.0 165.0,104.0 131.0,70.0\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\"/><polygon points=\"270.0,36.0 302.3,59.5 290.0,97.5 250.0,97.5 237.7,59.5\" fill=\"#fff3e0\"/><polygon points=\"60.0,176.0 89.4,193.0 89.4,227.0 60.0,244.0 30.6,227.0 30.6,193.0\" fill=\"#f3e5f5\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,176.0 191.6,188.8 198.1,217.6 179.8,240.6 150.2,240.6 131.9,217.6 138.4,188.8\" fill=\"#e0f7fa\"/><polygon points=\"270.0,176.0 294.0,186.0 304.0,210.0 294.0,234.0 270.0,244.0 246.0,234.0 236.0,210.0 246.0,186.0\" fill=\"#fce4ec\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,316.0 186.9,324.0 198.5,344.1 194.4,367.0 176.6,381.9 153.4,381.9 135.6,367.0 131.5,344.1 143.1,324.0\" fill=\"#fff9c4\"/></g><g font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1b5e20\"><text x=\"60\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Triangle</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">3 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Quadrilateral</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">4 sides</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pentagon</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"12\">5 sides</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hexagon</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">6 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heptagon</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">7 sides</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Octagon</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\">8 sides</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"402\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\">Enneagon</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"418\" font-size=\"12\">9 sides</text></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 12</b>: Polygons with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Seven polygons, with 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 sides, labelled by the number of their sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 200\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An octagon with eight sides\"><polygon points=\"180.0,25.0 233.0,47.0 255.0,100.0 233.0,153.0 180.0,175.0 127.0,153.0 105.0,100.0 127.0,47.0\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" font-size=\"38\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2e7d32\">8</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 13</b>: An octagon has 8 sides. Each side is one unit of our group of eight.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "An octagon with eight sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 150\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five octagons, each with eight sides\"><g stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"64.0,37.0 90.9,48.1 102.0,75.0 90.9,101.9 64.0,113.0 37.1,101.9 26.0,75.0 37.1,48.1\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\"/><polygon points=\"192.0,37.0 218.9,48.1 230.0,75.0 218.9,101.9 192.0,113.0 165.1,101.9 154.0,75.0 165.1,48.1\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\"/><polygon points=\"320.0,37.0 346.9,48.1 358.0,75.0 346.9,101.9 320.0,113.0 293.1,101.9 282.0,75.0 293.1,48.1\" fill=\"#fff3e0\" stroke=\"#ef6c00\"/><polygon points=\"448.0,37.0 474.9,48.1 486.0,75.0 474.9,101.9 448.0,113.0 421.1,101.9 410.0,75.0 421.1,48.1\" fill=\"#f3e5f5\" stroke=\"#6a1b9a\"/><polygon points=\"576.0,37.0 602.9,48.1 614.0,75.0 602.9,101.9 576.0,113.0 549.1,101.9 538.0,75.0 549.1,48.1\" fill=\"#fce4ec\" stroke=\"#ad1457\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 14</b>: Five octagons of the same size, each with eight sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Five octagons of the same size, each with eight sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of eight:** 8 × N = N + N + N + N + N + N + N + N. Counting by eights gives 8, 16, 24, 32, 40...\n\nFigure 14 shows five octagons: five groups of eight give 40. For example, 8 × 9 = 72.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "One last table remains after this one: the table of nine.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_table_nine",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Table of Nine\n\nNow, let us count 9 by 9. An enneagon (also called a nonagon) has nine sides, as Figure 15 shows.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 360 210\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An enneagon with nine sides\"><polygon points=\"180.0,27.0 230.1,45.2 256.8,91.5 247.5,144.0 206.7,178.3 153.3,178.3 112.5,144.0 103.2,91.5 129.9,45.2\" fill=\"#fff3e0\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" font-size=\"38\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2e7d32\">9</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 15</b>: An enneagon has 9 sides. Each side is one unit of our group of nine.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "An enneagon with nine sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 150\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five enneagons, each with nine sides\"><g stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"64.0,37.0 88.4,45.9 101.4,68.4 96.9,94.0 77.0,110.7 51.0,110.7 31.1,94.0 26.6,68.4 39.6,45.9\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\"/><polygon points=\"192.0,37.0 216.4,45.9 229.4,68.4 224.9,94.0 205.0,110.7 179.0,110.7 159.1,94.0 154.6,68.4 167.6,45.9\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\"/><polygon points=\"320.0,37.0 344.4,45.9 357.4,68.4 352.9,94.0 333.0,110.7 307.0,110.7 287.1,94.0 282.6,68.4 295.6,45.9\" fill=\"#fff3e0\" stroke=\"#ef6c00\"/><polygon points=\"448.0,37.0 472.4,45.9 485.4,68.4 480.9,94.0 461.0,110.7 435.0,110.7 415.1,94.0 410.6,68.4 423.6,45.9\" fill=\"#f3e5f5\" stroke=\"#6a1b9a\"/><polygon points=\"576.0,37.0 600.4,45.9 613.4,68.4 608.9,94.0 589.0,110.7 563.0,110.7 543.1,94.0 538.6,68.4 551.6,45.9\" fill=\"#fce4ec\" stroke=\"#ad1457\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 16</b>: Five enneagons of the same size, each with nine sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Five enneagons of the same size, each with nine sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **Table of nine:** 9 × N = N + N + N + N + N + N + N + N + N. Counting by nines gives 9, 18, 27, 36, 45...\n\nFigure 16 shows five enneagons of the same size: five groups of nine give 45 (9 × 5 = 45).",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A special shortcut for the table of nine: add the digits of each two-digit product. For 9 × 8 = 72, 7 + 2 = 9; for 9 × 6 = 54, 5 + 4 = 9. The digits always add up to 9. That completes our walk from 0 to 9.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_glance",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Ten Tables at a Glance",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The table below summarizes the ten tables. Use it as a quick reference when you need to check a product.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th>Table</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td><b>0</b></td><td>0 × N = 0. Counting by zero always gives 0.</td></tr><tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>1 × N = N. Counting by ones gives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5...</td></tr><tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>2 × N = N + N. Counting by twos gives 2, 4, 6, 8, 10...</td></tr><tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>3 × N = N + N + N. Counting by threes gives 3, 6, 9, 12, 15...</td></tr><tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>4 × N = N + N + N + N. Counting by fours gives 4, 8, 12, 16, 20...</td></tr><tr><td><b>5</b></td><td>5 × N = N + N + N + N + N. Counting by fives gives 5, 10, 15, 20, 25...</td></tr><tr><td><b>6</b></td><td>6 × N = N added six times. Counting by sixes gives 6, 12, 18, 24, 30...</td></tr><tr><td><b>7</b></td><td>7 × N = N added seven times. Counting by sevens gives 7, 14, 21, 28, 35...</td></tr><tr><td><b>8</b></td><td>8 × N = N added eight times. Counting by eights gives 8, 16, 24, 32, 40...</td></tr><tr><td><b>9</b></td><td>9 × N = N added nine times. Counting by nines gives 9, 18, 27, 36, 45...</td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: The ten multiplication tables at a glance.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_formula",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The General Formula",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "All ten tables can be written with one formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>r</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: The general formula of the multiplication tables.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this formula:\n\n- **r** is the **product**, the answer of the multiplication.\n- **N** is the number being counted; it can be any number.\n- **a** is the factor that names the table: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 or 9.\n\nBecause the order of the factors does not change the product, we can also write **a × N = N × a**. For example, with **a** = 8 and **N** = 9, both 8 × 9 and 9 × 8 give 72.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We walked through the ten multiplication tables one section at a time, from 0 to 9, and saw that they are not ten separate tricks. They all come from the same idea: adding the same number many times.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- **0 and 1** are special: 0 × N = 0 and 1 × N = N.\n- **2 to 9** are counting sequences: count by twos, threes, fours, fives, sixes, sevens, eights and nines.\n- **The general formula** r = a × N summarizes every table.\n- **Commutativity** means that a × N = N × a, so the order of the factors does not change the product.",
               "source": null,
